          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I have asthma and I use a blue inhaler.",
          "answer": "A 'blue inhaler' is typically a reliever inhaler, used for immediate relief of asthma symptoms. It works to open up the airways when you are experiencing symptoms like breathlessness.\n\nIts role in asthma management is to be used as needed to alleviate sudden symptoms. This is different from a 'controller' inhaler, which contains anti-inflammatory medicine and is taken regularly to prevent symptoms from occurring in the first place. Understanding the difference between these two types of inhalers is important for managing asthma effectively (General clinical reference and respiratory medicine educational summary).\n\nIf an asthma attack does not respond to reliever treatment, or returns within a few hours, prompt medical assessment is advised (General clinical reference (educational summary)).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
